    "evidence_text": "Genetics of obesity in adult adoptees and their biological siblings.\n\nIn full siblings body mass index (kg/m2) significantly increased with weight of the adoptees.\n\nGenetic analysis of human obesity in an Italian sample.\n\nOur conclusions are that genetic factors are certainly present.\n\nSeveral analyses suggest the presence of a dominant major gene with weak effect.\n\nObesity as a medical problem.\n\nIn particular, obesity is associated with diabetes mellitus, coronary heart disease, certain forms of cancer, and sleep-breathing disorders.\n\nThe global epidemic of obesity results from a combination of genetic susceptibility, increased availability of high-energy foods and decreased requirement for physical activity in modern society.\n\nObesity should no longer be regarded simply as a cosmetic problem affecting certain individuals, but an epidemic that threatens global well being.\n\nCongenital leptin deficiency is associated with severe early-onset obesity in humans.\n\nWe know of no equivalent evidence regarding leptin's role in the control of fat mass in humans.\n\nWe have examined two severely obese children who are members of the same highly consanguineous pedigree.\n\nThe links between obesity in parents and their offspring and the role of genes and a shared environment are not completely understood.\n\nThis was associated with changes in leptin and adiponectin expression in white adipose tissue.\n\nMaternal obesity epigenetically alters visceral fat progenitor cell properties in male offspring mice.\n\nKEY POINTS Maternal obesity reduces adipogenic progenitor density in offspring adipose tissue.\n\nABSTRACT Maternal obesity (MO) programs offspring obesity and metabolic disorders, although the underlying mechanisms remain poorly defined.\n\nProgenitor cells are the source of new adipocytes.\n\nFamilial obesity and leanness.\n\nNearly all subjects with familial obesity or leanness had no overt metabolic or pharmacological explanations for their body habitus.\n\nObesity is a multifactorial disease with a marked genetic component.\n\nFurthermore, the distribution of fat shows a stronger heritable tendency compared with total body fat.\n\nThe candidate genes examined were insulin receptor, insulin sensitive glucose transporter and insulin.\n\nNo such associations were found with the other candidate genes studied.\n\nAn adoption study of human obesity.\n\nWe conclude that genetic influences have an important role in determining human fatness in adults, whereas the family environment alone has no apparent effect.\n\nDietary fat and obesity: evidence from epidemiology.\n\nThe epidemiological evidence that a high-fat diet promotes the development of obesity is considered suggestive but not definitive.\n\nCross-sectional studies are generally in agreement that the concentration of fat in the diet is positively associated with relative weight.\n\nProspective studies of diet in relation to subsequent weight change give inconsistent results.\n\nThe experimental evidence on this relationship is more conclusive than the epidemiological evidence, although biological mechanisms remain controversial.",
